%description:

RFC 9000 -- stream identifiers.

Checks RFC9000-STR-1 (description of the encoding, normative), from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9000/catalog.md. The English procedure and mockup are in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks.md#stream-identifiers; the program step below maps onto
all three of its expected observations at once, because all three describe the very same STREAM
frame (its existence, and two bit conditions on the one streamId field it carries) -- the engine
matches an event once and advances, so a later step cannot re-examine an already-matched event;
folding same-event observations into one step (with the comment naming all of them) is the
convention this suite already uses (see e.g. Rfc791DatagramDelivery.test's step 1, which covers
three catalog IDs on one event). Mockup mapping: host A = client, host B = server.

Scenario: examples/quic/basic3's own network and QuicClient/QuicDiscardServer apps, unmodified --
this check does not need the 4000-octet common-mockup block (checks.md's own Procedure for this
check only asks for "one bidirectional stream" with "data" on it, not a specific size), so
QuicClient's hardcoded pair of writes (1000B then 500B, both explicitly tagged streamId=0 in
QuicClient.cc) is the simplest, least contrived source of "the client's first stream": nothing in
this test's ini chooses that number, it is the reference example's own fixed behaviour.

*** Finding, contrary to the "expect this to fail" hint in the task brief: the assertion PASSES. ***
The observed identifier is streamId=0, which is even (bit0=0: client-initiated, correct per
RFC9000-STR-1) and has bit1=0 (bidirectional, also "correct"). But this is coincidence, not
conformance: Quic.ned's own "Missing bits" section states plainly that "[this module does] not
distinguish [stream types by opener/directionality]. All streams can be used bidirectional" -- and
tracing the code confirms there is no encoding logic to violate. QuicClient.cc's
socketEstablished() sets the id with a bare `tags.addTagIfAbsent<QuicStreamReq>()->setStreamID(0)`
(and QuicTrafficgen.cc's getStreamId() just returns its "generatorId" parameter verbatim: "return
generatorId;"). EstablishedConnectionState::processSendAppCommand() reads that tag's value with
`pkt->getTag<QuicStreamReq>()->getStreamID()` and hands it straight to
Connection::newStreamData()/findOrCreateStream(), which does `new Stream(streamId, this, stats)`
with no validation, transformation, or role/directionality bookkeeping anywhere in between. The
wire value is whatever raw integer the application chose. Had QuicClient (or a QuicTrafficgen
generator) been written to pass streamId=1, this exact test would observe an odd, unidirectional-
looking identifier from a stream that is, per the model's own documentation, always actually
bidirectional -- and the check would rightly fail. This test asserts the encoding faithfully as
checks.md states it (not weakened or inverted, per this suite's hard rule); it happens to pass
only because 0 is what the reference client app happens to hardcode as its first stream ID, not
because the model enforces or even represents RFC9000-STR-1's encoding.

Implementation note: as in the other QUIC tests in this suite, evalPacketField()/.packet("quic....")
cannot see inside a QUIC packet (no protocol dissector is registered for it -- see
QuicChunks.h and Rfc9000ConnectionEstablishment.test's %description for the empirical evidence);
this test reads the STREAM frame's streamId directly off the packet's own chunk tree via
QuicChunks.h's findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>().

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "QuicChunks.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9000_stream_identifiers")
        // Expected observations 1, 2 and 3, all on the one event: host A sends a datagram
        // whose QUIC packet carries a STREAM frame (confirms the stimulus); the stream
        // identifier's low bit is 0 (client-initiated, RFC9000-STR-1); and its second bit
        // is 0 (bidirectional, RFC9000-STR-1). See the %description for the observed value
        // (0) and why it passes only by coincidence, not by the model enforcing the
        // encoding.
        .once(on("client.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      auto sf = findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(c.event.packet);
                      if (sf == nullptr)
                          return false;
                      uint64_t streamId = sf->getStreamId();
                      return (streamId & 0x1) == 0        // low bit: client-initiated
                          && (streamId & 0x2) == 0;        // second bit: bidirectional
                  })
                  .describe("host A's first STREAM frame: an even streamId whose second bit is 0")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9000_stream_identifiers compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks.md's "Common mockup":
// host A (client) -- path P -- host B (server).
network QuicNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        client: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        server: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        client.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> server.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = QuicNet
sim-time-limit = 5s
seed-set = 0

# examples/quic/basic3's own client/server apps, unmodified: the client opens a connection,
# writes 1000B then 500B (both on stream 0, hardcoded in QuicClient.cc), and closes.
**.client.hasQuic = true
**.client.numApps = 1
**.client.app[*].typename = "QuicClient"
**.client.app[*].localPort = 1001
**.client.app[*].connectPort = 1000
**.client.app[*].connectAddress = "server"

**.server.hasQuic = true
**.server.numApps = 1
**.server.app[*].typename = "QuicDiscardServer"
**.server.app[*].localAddress = ""
**.server.app[*].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9000_stream_identifiers: PASS
